# The counter-proof register. One line per check: what made that one
# check go red, or that it is still owed.
#
# A check nobody has ever seen fail is not known to check anything. In
# one day seventeen were found here that had been green for months while
# testing nothing. So the thing a check is about gets broken on purpose,
# the test is run against that, and the red line is kept here word for
# word. Without the line an entry is a claim; with it, it is evidence.
#
# Two kinds of row, both tab-separated:
#
#   test   when   the check   what was broken   the red line
#   open   test   the check
#
# The second kind is the census of what is still owed, and it is the
# ratchet: the number of checks owing a counter-proof may fall, never
# rise. source_checks_proved_test.py turns red naming the checks that
# raised it.
#
# The number jumped once, on 31.8.2026, from 100 to 2498. It counted
# tests without an entry before and counts checks without one now.
# Nothing was proved that day and nothing came undone: 46 tests had an
# entry that covered one of their checks and said nothing about the
# sixty-four beside it, and the count had been understating the debt by
# all of them. A rise it is not, an honest floor it is.
#
# What no machine here sees: a check that keeps its wording while its
# claim changes -- a limit moved, a comparison turned round -- keeps a
# row that no longer proves anything, and only whoever makes the change
# catches it. Nor can it tell a new check from a reworded one when an
# open row is written for it by hand. So a new check gets a finished
# entry, never an open row.
#
# A row is tied to one check, and it names it by the wording of that
# check -- the first argument of its `check(...)`, word for word,
# leading blanks and all. Two things follow, and both are the point of
# this file:
#
#   Rewording one check voids that one row and leaves its neighbours
#   alone. A counter-proof from yesterday says nothing about a check
#   rewritten today, but it goes on saying everything about the
#   sixty-four beside it.
#
#   The wording alone is not the key: nineteen wordings stand in more
#   than one test ("English label", "and it says why"). The test name
#   beside it is what tells them apart.
#
# The name is written straight, not held to: a test can be renamed, and
# its rows are found again by the wordings they carry -- a renamed test
# keeps every row, and the new name is written in the next time the
# suite runs.
#
# The open rows are written by the suite. Whoever proves a check takes
# its open row, puts the three further fields on it, and the row is an
# entry. Nothing else has to be worked out by hand, and no fingerprint
# has to be fetched from a run any more.
#
# A row for a check that no longer stands in its test is reported, and
# so is a row for a test that is gone. A test the builder set aside is
